\section*{Pointwise convergence of fourfold ergodic averages under mixing}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Manuscript 1 (family 154, 2026-10-04): For every invertible mixing transformation, consecutive multiple ergodic averages of any fixed finite length are claimed to have an almost-everywhere limit equal to the product of the input integrals, without a mixing rate. Manuscript 2 (family 154, 2026-10-04): Fourfold averages at times $n,2n,3n,4n$ are claimed to converge pointwise to the product of integrals for every invertible mixing system and bounded inputs on arbitrary probability spaces.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} Let $(X,\mathcal F,\mu)$ be any probability space with an invertible bimeasurable measure-preserving transformation $T$. Assume mixing, meaning $\mu(A\cap T^{-m}B)\to\mu(A)\mu(B)$ as $|m|\to\infty$ for all measurable $A,B$. OpenAI claims, for every $n\ge2$ and every fixed bounded measurable $f_1,\ldots,f_n$,
\[
\frac1N\sum_{k=1}^N\prod_{j=1}^n f_j(T^{jk}x)\longrightarrow\prod_{j=1}^n\int_X f_j\,d\mu
\quad\text{for almost every }x.
\]
The limit uses all positive integers $N$, requires no mixing rate or standardness of the probability space, and its null exceptional set may depend on the functions.

\paragraph{Mathematical claim 2.} For any probability space and any invertible bimeasurable measure-preserving mixing $T$, OpenAI claims for every fixed $f_1,\ldots,f_4\in L^\infty(\mu)$ that
\[
\frac1N\sum_{k=1}^N\prod_{j=1}^4 f_j(T^{jk}x)\longrightarrow\prod_{j=1}^4\int_X f_j\,d\mu
\quad\mu\text{-almost everywhere}.
\]
Mixing means $\mu(A\cap T^{-m}B)\to\mu(A)\mu(B)$ for every measurable $A,B$ as $|m|\to\infty$; no quantitative rate or standard-space assumption is imposed, and convergence is along all positive $N$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 154, 2026-10-04): A minimal nonzero centered correlation makes smaller selected spatial lists independent. Conditional-expectation energy saturation stabilizes extensions, and a square joining array is regrouped to create an additional output-only slot; maximality of that slot count gives a contradiction using only the existing at-most-three-slot pointwise theorem.

Manuscript 2 (family 154, 2026-10-04): Three-slot oscillation estimates and the companion multiple-mixing theorem supply the analytic inputs. Selected joining laws are packed into iterated arrays, where orthogonality bounds the number of extra-coordinate slots; saturated conditional laws and reanchoring increase that number, contradicting its maximal choice.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 154, 2026-10-04): The selected question is contained in the manuscript statement summarized here. Its broader range or additional conclusion therefore gives a claimed proof of the question as a specialization.

Manuscript 2 (family 154, 2026-10-04): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

